Let \( \Phi : \Sigma 	o \Sigma' \). Let \( L \) be a \( \Sigma \)-language and let \( L' \) be a \( \Sigma' \)-language. Then a \emph{\( \Phi \)-morphism} of languages \( L \xrightarrow\Phi L' \) consists of a map \[ 
ewoperator{onFunction} : \prod_{n : \mathbb N} \prod_{A : \Fin n 	o \Sigma} \prod_{B : \Sigma} 
ewoperator{Functions}_L(n, A, B) 	o 
ewoperator{Functions}_{L'}(\Phi \circ A, \Phi(B)) \] and a map \[ 
ewoperator{onRelation} : \prod_{n : \mathbb N} \prod_{A : \Fin n 	o \Sigma} 
ewoperator{Relations}_L(n, A) 	o 
ewoperator{Functions}_{L'}(\Phi \circ A) \] If \( L, L' \) are \( \Sigma \)-languages, we may simply say that a \emph{morphism} of languages \( L 	o L' \) is a \( \id_\Sigma \)-morphism \( L \xrightarrow{\id_\Sigma} L' \). ootnote{It is crucial that \( \id \circ f \equiv f \circ \id \equiv f \) definitionally.}
